WEBVTT

00:00:00.606 --> 00:00:01.736
Hi, I'm Tucker.

00:00:02.146 --> 00:00:05.602
This is a short walk through PA.gov, the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania's website,

00:00:05.602 --> 00:00:07.108
and the part I played in it.

00:00:07.778 --> 00:00:10.889
I owned the UX architecture and led a team of four designers.

00:00:11.189 --> 00:00:14.434
I also set direction for our visual design lead,

00:00:14.434 --> 00:00:15.658
a fellow associate creative director.

00:00:15.809 --> 00:00:18.249
We worked closely with the governor's digital team.

00:00:19.264 --> 00:00:21.900
The program covered sixty-two websites across forty-three agencies,

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.830
each organized around the agency that ran it.

00:00:26.113 --> 00:00:28.547
The governor had committed to "no wrong door."

00:00:28.717 --> 00:00:30.429
The sites were doing the opposite.

00:00:31.609 --> 00:00:34.265
Our early research showed what that meant for people.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.346
Someone applying for benefits for the first time

00:00:37.346 --> 00:00:39.775
is often coming off a financial shock.

00:00:39.913 --> 00:00:42.369
They search, get overwhelmed by complex information,

00:00:42.369 --> 00:00:46.097
and sometimes land on unscrupulous sites before they find the right one.

00:00:47.383 --> 00:00:50.248
For people already receiving benefits, the hard part was keeping them,

00:00:50.248 --> 00:00:52.633
with renewals that were easy to miss.

00:00:53.097 --> 00:00:54.760
Both pointed to the same starting point:

00:00:54.975 --> 00:00:58.358
help people find the right service quickly, without knowing which agency owns it.

00:00:58.844 --> 00:01:01.439
Here's the structure, simplified.

00:01:01.772 --> 00:01:04.671
Services come first, as task-based entry points,

00:01:05.082 --> 00:01:07.376
with the content supplied by each agency.

00:01:08.439 --> 00:01:13.061
Then every agency gets the same landing page, with the same seven sections.

00:01:13.335 --> 00:01:16.619
Visitors learn the pattern once, and it works on every agency's site.

00:01:18.372 --> 00:01:22.563
The core idea was to start from the resident, not the org chart.

00:01:22.848 --> 00:01:25.051
This is the Find Services page.

00:01:25.051 --> 00:01:28.050
Next to agency and service type, there are life-event filters.

00:01:28.639 --> 00:01:31.456
Choose "Navigating Unemployment," and you see services from

00:01:31.456 --> 00:01:34.356
every agency at once, not just one department.

00:01:35.206 --> 00:01:37.530
That works because of a change underneath.

00:01:37.962 --> 00:01:44.012
Content used to live in folders, one tree per agency.

00:01:44.434 --> 00:01:47.676
We moved it to tags for agency, topic, and life event.

00:01:48.552 --> 00:01:52.533
Now one service page can show up

00:01:52.533 --> 00:01:55.328
wherever a resident might look for it.

00:01:56.504 --> 00:01:58.572
We also gave every service the same shape.

00:01:58.768 --> 00:02:02.224
It opens with what the service is and how to start,

00:02:02.717 --> 00:02:06.970
then an on-page guide to eligibility, filing, and next steps.

00:02:08.794 --> 00:02:10.548
The ways to act sit side by side: online,

00:02:10.908 --> 00:02:14.686
by phone, or by videophone for people who use sign language.

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:17.440
One decision is worth mentioning.

00:02:17.919 --> 00:02:20.234
We first planned to organize around life events,

00:02:20.234 --> 00:02:23.821
but no one on the client side was going to own that content.

00:02:23.821 --> 00:02:26.579
So we kept our recommendations simpler, and put the effort

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:29.870
into examples and guidance agencies could follow on their own.

00:02:31.141 --> 00:02:32.490
That turned out to matter.

00:02:32.842 --> 00:02:35.385
Agencies weren't required to adopt the new structure,

00:02:35.574 --> 00:02:37.212
and all but a handful did.

00:02:37.634 --> 00:02:41.835
Across the program, the accessibility audit score went from 37 to 95 percent.

00:02:42.264 --> 00:02:43.854
Those numbers come from a lot of people's work.

00:02:43.854 --> 00:02:47.375
My part was the structure underneath, and the team that designed it.

00:02:48.831 --> 00:02:49.930
Thanks for watching.
